---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/fossils-and-geologic-time">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Geologic time is <strong>the deepest idea in science: 4.54 billion years, measured</strong>.
    To feel the scale: if Earth's history were a single year, life appears in March,
    complex animals in November, dinosaurs in mid-December, and the whole of recorded human
    history occupies the last half-minute before midnight on 31 December. Grasping that
    ratio is not a party trick; it is the prerequisite for understanding evolution
    (selection needs those billions of generations), plate tectonics (continents drift
    centimetres per year — arithmetic only works across eons), and climate (CO₂ cycles
    measured in hundreds of thousands of years). The logic that built the number came in
    two steps — ordering by relationships (which layer is older?) and dating by physics
    (exactly how old?) — and fossils supplied the first correlation system decades before
    isotopes supplied the numbers. This lesson covers both systems: relative dating,
    absolute dating, the timescale they produced, and the fossils that gave it its
    vocabulary.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="deep time, relative ordering, radiometric clocks, the timescale" widget="equationStep" config={timeCfg} />

  <h2>Relative dating: ordering without numbers</h2>
  <p>
    The 17th–19th centuries solved most of the ordering with a handful of principles so
    sound they still open every geology course. <strong>Superposition:</strong> in an
    undisturbed stack, lower layers are older — sediment settles downward.
    <strong>Original horizontality:</strong> sediments deposit in flat layers, so tilted or
    folded strata were deformed <em>after</em> deposition. <strong>Cross-cutting
    relationships:</strong> a fault or intrusion must be younger than the rocks it cuts
    through — you cannot break what is not there yet. <strong>Inclusions:</strong> a rock
    fragment inside another is older than its host. <strong>Faunal succession</strong>
    (William Smith, 1799, canal surveyor): fossils appear in the strata in a definite,
    repeatable vertical order — the same sequence in Wales and in Normandy — so fossil
    content correlates rocks across continents. Add <strong>unconformities</strong> —
    surfaces where missing time is recorded (erosion, or deposition that simply stopped) —
    and geologists could stack every rock unit on Earth into one chronological column
    <em>without knowing a single date in years</em>. That column was complete enough to
    name its chapters (Cambrian, Jurassic) a century before anyone could put numbers on
    them.
  </p>

  <h2>Absolute dating: physics writes the numbers</h2>
  <p>
    Radiometric dating exploits one of physics' most reliable facts:
    <strong>unstable isotopes decay at a fixed, environment-independent rate</strong>,
    conventionally quoted as a <strong>half-life</strong> — the time for half of any
    parent population to convert to daughter. Since the rate is indifferent to
    temperature, pressure and chemistry, a mineral that closed its lattice at
    crystallisation (locking parent in, daughter out) is a clock started at its birth.
    Worked example: a zircon contains uranium-238 (half-life 4.47 billion years) and
    analysis shows one quarter of the original U-238 remains — two half-lives have
    elapsed, so the zircon grew ~8.9 billion years... which is impossible, of course, and
    the check is instructive: every method comes with assumptions about the starting
    composition, and geologists date the <em>same rock by two or more independent
    decay systems</em> (U–Pb, K–Ar, Rb–Sr) — concordant clocks validate each other, and
    discordant ones announce a re-heated rock whose clock was reset. The roster: carbon-14
    (half-life 5,730 years) dates organic material to ~50,000 years — archaeology's clock
    (its inventor, Willard Libby, won the 1960 Nobel); K–Ar and Ar–Ar date volcanic rocks;
    U–Pb in zircon is the champion, with its dual independent decay chains and
    lead-rejecting crystal — grains from Western Australia dated to 4.4 billion years are
    the oldest known terrestrial material. And the <strong>age of the Earth, 4.54 ± 0.05
    billion years</strong>, comes from Clair Patterson's 1956 dating of meteorites: Earth
    rocks recycle (the rock-cycle lesson), so the planet's own surface is younger than the
    planet — but meteorites, leftovers of solar-system formation, are not. Dating them
    dated the system Earth formed in. (Patterson also discovered, mid-project, that his
    samples were lead-contaminated — from petrol — and his decade-long campaign against
    leaded fuel is one of science's great public-health stories hiding inside a dating
    paper.)
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "carbon-14 dating proves the age of the Earth / of
    dinosaurs." Carbon-14 is geology's wristwatch, not its calendar: useful only to
    ~50,000 years, and it dates <em>organic</em> material. Dinosaur bones are dated by the
    volcanic ash layers above and below them (U–Pb), never by the bone itself.
  </p>

  <h2>The geologic time scale: measured chapters</h2>
  <p>
    The timescale is the union of the two systems: the <em>order</em> from relative dating,
    the <em>numbers</em> from radiometry. Its hierarchy runs eons → eras → periods →
    epochs. The <strong>Precambrian</strong> (Hadean, Archean, Proterozoic eons) covers the
    first 4 billion years — planet formation, first life, oxygenation — and, despite its
    length, is subdivided coarsely because fossils are sparse. The <strong>Phanerozoic</strong>
    ("visible life", the last 539 million years) is finely divided because fossils are
    abundant: <strong>Paleozoic</strong> (Cambrian explosion, fish, land plants, ending in
    the Great Dying), <strong>Mesozoic</strong> (dinosaurs, first birds and flowers, ended
    by the asteroid), <strong>Cenozoic</strong> (mammals, grasslands, ice ages, us). Two
    features make the scale honest. First, boundaries are <em>events, not
    round numbers</em>: the Cretaceous–Paleogene boundary is 66.0 million years ago because
    that is when Chicxulub's iridium layer was laid down, dated by the ash around it.
    Second, the units are defined by <em>type sections</em> — a real rock layer at a real
    place (usually in Europe, for historical reasons: "Cambrian" from Wales) — and the
    International Commission on Stratigraphy still ratifies each boundary with a "golden
    spike" in the reference rock. Dates are measured; the names are conventional; the
    framework is global.
  </p>

  <h2>Fossils: the instruments and the archive</h2>
  <p>
    A fossil is any trace of past life — and how it forms determines what it can tell you.
    <strong>Preservation is rare and biased:</strong> it needs hard parts (shell, bone,
    teeth), rapid burial, and low oxygen — which is why soft-bodied creatures are
    underrepresented (the Cambrian's Burgess Shale, where soft parts survived, rewrites the
    period's diversity every time a new quarry is worked) and why we have more fossils of
    clams than of caterpillars. The modes matter: <strong>permineralisation</strong>
    (minerals infuse bone and wood — petrified forests), <strong>molds and casts</strong>
    (the shell dissolves, leaving a mould, or sediment fills it — a cast),
    <strong>amber</strong> (whole insects, DNA's best chance), <strong>carbonisation</strong>
    (thin films of carbon — the Burgess Shale's technique), and <strong>trace fossils</strong>
    (footprints, burrows — behaviour without anatomy, as at Laetoli, where hominin
    footprints 3.6 million years old record two individuals walking side by side).
    <strong>Index fossils</strong> are species chosen for correlation: wide geographic
    range, short tenure, abundance — trilobites and ammonites built the Paleozoic and
    Mesozoic timescales before any isotope existed, and microfossils (foraminifera) do
    the same work today in oil exploration, dating the rock strata drillers pass through.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read at scale, the archive records life's catastrophes: <strong>five mass
    extinctions</strong> — end-Ordovician, Late Devonian, end-Permian ("the Great Dying":
    ~96% of marine species, likely via Siberian-Traps volcanism and ocean anoxia),
    end-Triassic, and end-Cretaceous (Chicxulub: iridium anomaly, shocked quartz, the
    crater found exactly where the physics said it should be). Each reset evolution's
    course — the mammals' rise <em>is</em> the end-Cretaceous aftermath — and the current,
    human-driven sixth extinction is measured against this baseline: current rates run
    100–1,000× the fossil background, which is why palaeobiologists, not just ecologists,
    keep the books.
  </p>

  <p>
    A worked correlation shows the machinery. Three quarries on opposite sides of a
    basin expose limestone beds with no visible way to line them up. Quarry A's bed 3
    holds <em>Ammonites parkinsoni</em>, known (from a century of records) to span only
    a narrow slice of the Jurassic; quarry B's bed 7 has the same species; quarry C's
    bed 2 has it too, plus a distinctive foraminiferan. All three beds are therefore
    the same age, regardless of their local numbers or thicknesses — and if a thin
    volcanic ash layer sits just above the ammonite in quarry B, a U–Pb date on its
    zircons (say 168.3 ± 1.0 Ma) stamps all three beds. That two-step — index fossil
    for the correlation, ash bed for the number — is exactly how the era-defining
    boundaries were fixed: faunal succession draws the lines, radiometric dating pins
    the pins, and each anchors the other's errors.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The intellectual revolution deserves its own paragraphs. <strong>Deep time had to be
    invented:</strong> until Hutton (1788) — who watched slow sedimentation at Siccar Point
    and realised an unconformity under his feet represented "no vestige of a beginning" —
    and Lyell (1830), whose <em>Principles</em> preached uniformitarianism ("the present is
    the key to the past"), the West assumed a 6,000-year Earth. Lord Kelvin's late-1800s
    cooling calculation (~20–100 million years, by physics as it then stood) was the best
    scientific objection — and it failed only because radioactivity, undiscovered in his
    lifetime, was both adding heat he did not know about and providing, in decay, the very
    clock that eventually settled the question. Geology invented deep time; Darwin
    explicitly needed it for selection; physics nearly vetoed it; radioactivity arbitrated.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quantitative edges: the radiometric <em>timescale</em> and the relative <em>record</em>
    fit together with a precision that surprises outsiders — the 66.043-million-year
    Chicxulub date carries uncertainties of tens of thousands of years (~0.05%), and the
    astronomical-tuning method (reading Earth's orbital wobbles out of sediment cycles)
    now dates Cenozoic epochs almost to orbital cycles. The record's known bias cuts the
    other way: the <strong>incompleteness of the archive</strong> means most time is
    missing from any one place (unconformities are time you will never read), so
    palaeontologists correct for the "Pull of the Recent" (the youngest record is
    artificially richer) and for rock-volume effects when counting diversity through
    time. Open frontiers: pushing Precambrian dating finer (biomarkers and isotope
    systems beyond zircon), dating the first life claims (the 3.5–3.7 Ga stromatolites and
    their contested chemistry), and formally defining an "Anthropocene" epoch — a live
    debate about whether human impact has left a boundary layer worthy of a golden spike.
    The timescale, in other words, is still being written — by the same two-step method
    that wrote it: order first, then measure.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hutton (1788); Lyell (1830); Patterson, Geochimica et Cosmochimica Acta (1956); Gradstein et al., the International Chronostratigraphic Chart; Renne et al. on the K–Pg boundary date.</p>
</TopicPage>
